<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the hardened runtime has to be asked to allow.

  Notarisation requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime forbids by default
  several things the .NET runtime does as a matter of course. Each of these is a hole in
  that protection, so each is here for a stated reason rather than because a forum post
  listed it:

  * allow-jit — CoreCLR compiles IL to machine code at runtime and needs pages that are
    both writable and executable. Without this the app dies on launch.

  * allow-unsigned-executable-memory — the same requirement in the older, blunter form.
    CoreCLR still trips the check that allow-jit alone does not cover, and the failure is
    an immediate crash rather than anything diagnosable.

  * disable-library-validation — Avalonia loads native libraries (Skia, HarfBuzz) that
    ship inside the bundle. They are signed here with the same identity, so this ought to
    be unnecessary; it is included because library validation also rejects loads that
    happen through dlopen paths the check cannot attribute, and a launcher that fails to
    draw is worse than one entitlement broader than strictly required.

  Worth revisiting: try removing disable-library-validation and see whether a notarised
  build still starts. If it does, drop it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
